WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
kay.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.310
Let's do some audience questions for the week.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.310
Some, uh, good ones.

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:05.070
I'm looking forward to answering today.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.650
First one is from [inaudible].

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:10.540
Hey David, why does your show have a mostly
male audience?

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.490
Well, this is actually, it's not just our
show.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:16.880
This is a general dynamic for shows like ours
in general.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.290
And this is something I've talked to a lot
of people about.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.800
I know a Sam Cedar and I have talked about
this and I've talked about it with a lot of

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.800
folks over at the young Turks and it just
sort of a tough nut to crack.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.950
And what it really comes down to is that fundamentally
online news and politics has a male audience.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:37.770
So it's not so much about the shows, it's
about the format.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:43.590
I don't have a single close female friend
I can think of that watches my show or shows

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:44.590
like mine.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:45.590
I mean period.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:48.970
They, you know, they're sort of tangentially
aware of my show because they see stuff on

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:50.530
Facebook for example.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:57.630
But nobody that I know who, um, uh, is, is
a woman regularly says, oh yeah, I watched

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.730
the young Turks or Sam Cedar or whatever.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:05.309
And it is really common for shows like the
David Pakman show to have anywhere from 60

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:06.930
to 80% male audiences.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:13.450
I think our podcast audience is, has a little
more, um, a female to male ratio in it.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.500
The youtube channel is more male than the
podcast, for example.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.580
Hard to get data from radio or from television.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.030
And part of it is the format, which is, it's
just a format that is more heavily watched

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.290
by men.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:32.360
Part of it is also story selection because,
um, if you look at studies about which stories

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.090
were more followed by men versus more followed
by women, when you look at from Pew Research,

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:43.800
for example, the news stories that were more
followed by women than men.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.670
It's all stories that we didn't cover.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:46.670
Okay.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:53.320
So the coal miners and Utah was a news story
that was followed more by women than by men.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.590
Um, contaminated pet food.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.619
We didn't cover it.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:02.900
Maybe it got a mention on the bonus show,
the Chinese toy recall, um, a variety of kidnappings

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.250
of kids that I don't even remember at this
point over the years.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:11.740
The story about drug resistant staph infection
spreading, we didn't cover or barely covered

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.300
any of those stories.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.010
So it's also story selection.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:20.540
If you look at stories like for example at
Venezuela, uh, the Supreme Court, immigration,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.390
Iran, those are stories that were more heavily
followed by men than by women.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:29.470
So in short, you've got to format that in
general is more watched by men than by women.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:34.960
And then you have stories selection within
our show, which also seems to not be including

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.060
some of those stories that were followed more
close closely by women.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.670
Uh, and that's sort of where you end up.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:42.670
Now.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.300
I am not considering it sort of a forgone
conclusion or sort of fait accompli.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:54.270
We are actively talking about how can we increase
our female following and uh, you know, there's

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.770
little things that I do.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:04.470
One is, uh, with voicemails when we get voicemails
from women, I make sure to try to get more

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.450
of those voices represented because it creates
this vicious circle.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:13.820
If everybody you hear leaving a voicemail
is male and your female, that might be a disincentive

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:14.990
to even call in.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.160
So, uh, same thing with phone calls.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.760
We've got some great female callers including
valve from Agawam who we love and when she

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.720
calls in we just try to, you know, include
more female voices and also with guests selection.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.940
There is actually a big discussion, um, that
happens in general when doing analyses of

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:39.720
news media that they're not only is there
a big corporate skew of so called experts

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.810
on news media, there's also a major male skew.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:50.220
And so, uh, since overwhelmingly the guests
that we get pitched our men, we also make

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:55.069
sure that for the guests that we decide to
go after and invite to the show that we're

00:03:55.069 --> 00:03:56.720
including a, uh, a mix.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.850
And we've had some really great female guest
as well.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.170
So that's what we're doing.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:05.440
But there are some sort of systemic issues
that explain a lot of why that is.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.879
Now if you say, why is it that online news
and politics does not appeal to women as much

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:11.879
as it does to men?

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:13.080
I don't have the answer.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.019
Um, and I'd be curious to hear from you what
you think might explain it

